/-!
# `cor:regular-nonmf-algebra`: the one upstream leaf it still inherits

`non_mf_groups_exist.tex`, line 2204, `\label{cor:regular-nonmf-algebra}`:

> The algebra $C^*_{\mathrm r}(Q)$ is separable, unital, simple, and
> generated by two unitaries.  It has a unique tracial state and stable rank
> one, and it is stably finite but not MF.

The corollary's own proof is `RegularAlgebraOfGroup`, with nothing assumed:
`printedRegularNonMFAlgebra_of_printedTorsionFreeTheorem` derives the whole
corollary from the printed form of `thm:torsion-free`.

The existence of the group `Q` is `thm:torsion-free` (line 284).  In the corpus,
`TorsionFreeGreendlingerLeaf.printedTorsionFreeTheorem_of_greendlinger` proves it
from exactly one hypothesis: `GGT.VanKampen.RelativeGreendlingerQuasiGeodesicLeastAreaStatement`
(Osin's Lemma 4.4 at least-area van Kampen diagrams, the Greendlinger step inside
Hull's Theorem 7.1).  The other inputs of that route (the relative isoperimetric
bridge, Kotowski--Ollivier, Hyde--Lodha) are closed theorems.  This module
records that dependency: its endpoint is conditional on that one leaf and
nothing else, so its census row stays `partial`.
-/

namespace GroupApproximation
namespace Manuscript
namespace NonMF
namespace OneSidedRegular

/-- **`cor:regular-nonmf-algebra`**, both sentences, for the group `Q` of
`thm:torsion-free`.  The corollary's own proof adds no hypothesis.  The only
one here is the upstream Greendlinger leaf of `thm:torsion-free`. -/
theorem regularNonMFAlgebra_of_greendlinger
    (hgreendlinger :
      GGT.VanKampen.RelativeGreendlingerQuasiGeodesicLeastAreaStatement.{0, 0, 0}) :
    TorsionFreePrinted.PrintedRegularNonMFAlgebra :=
  printedRegularNonMFAlgebra_of_printedTorsionFreeTheorem
    (TorsionFreeGreendlingerLeaf.printedTorsionFreeTheorem_of_greendlinger hgreendlinger)

end OneSidedRegular
end NonMF
end Manuscript
end GroupApproximation

#audit_axioms GroupApproximation.Manuscript.NonMF.OneSidedRegular.regularNonMFAlgebra_of_greendlinger
